events.text:
Now the horizon reads as a soft, hazy bank of wood — much better. It's subtle, maybe a touch too subtle/flat, but as a distant shore in evening vapour it's right. There's a faint residual ghost mark at top-left (around x 140-230, y 575) — a pale remnant of an old trunk. I'll deal with it later with the wood drawing (the new trunks will cover that area anyway) or a touch-up.

Now the main event: the grove. Without shake this time. Trunks:
- Start thin at the top, widen slightly toward the foot, slightly irregular.
- Value: mid-grey, cooler than the mist. Let me use pile: lead white 3.5, pale smalt 3.5, raw umber 3.2, medium 0.05 → ~0.6 value cool grey.
- Widths: filbert 6-9 units, pressure ~0.6-0.8 → mark ~4-6 units.
- Heights: vary, tops around y 570-660 (some higher, reaching into the pale band).
- Lean: slight.
- Feet: enter the water at y 790-850.

Then the three near trees: darker (value 0.42-0.5), width 8-10, with fine branches, tops up to y 540-580.

Let me be careful with the strokes: 2 segments per trunk, no shake, pressure ramps. Also to avoid the "rounded pill" look, the top segment should end (or begin) with low pressure. I'll draw from top → foot with pressure {0.25, 0.8} on the upper segment and {0.8, 0.9} on the lower, so the top is thin and dissolves.

Let me write the chunk with explicit per-tree data (my own drawn choices).

Grove (13): {fx, fy, w, ty, lean}
1. {139, 792, 7, 596, 8}
2. {211, 800, 6, 628, -6}
